<Base title="Projects" description="Anywhim, Whimtrade, and what Giacomo Mari is building now.">
  <h1>Projects</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Four things I have built, not in the order they happened. Those that no longer exist turned out to be the more useful of the list.
  </p>

  <h2>Anywhim</h2>
  <span class="label">Retail company · 2022–2024 · Italy</span>
  <p>
    A multi-site retail business in high-end fashion and resale, co-founded with Andrea and three more partners who joined later, Matteo, Davide and Michele,
    when I was 19. Over two and a half years it grew to four stores, a central warehouse, and administrative office, an online shop
    and a wholesale brand, reaching €1,000,000 in annual turnover. At peak we were seven people
    and I was responsible for the team.
  </p>
  <p>
    It closed in December 2024. I have written more about why elsewhere, but the short version
    is that a business cannot run without a vision.
    I learned more from the wind-down than from any of the growth.
  </p>

  <h3>Mantova, The Cozy Hometown</h3>
  {intros.mantova && <p class="shop-intro">{intros.mantova}</p>}
  <Slideshow slides={mantova} label="the Mantova store" />

  <h3>Cortina d’Ampezzo, The Queen of the Dolomites</h3>
  {intros.cortina && <p class="shop-intro">{intros.cortina}</p>}
  <Slideshow slides={cortina} label="the Cortina d’Ampezzo store" />

  <h3>Milano Marittima, Such a Fun Place</h3>
  {intros.milano && <p class="shop-intro">{intros.milano}</p>}
  <Slideshow slides={milano} label="the Milano Marittima store" />

  <h3>Riccione, The Green Pearl of Adriatic Sea</h3>
  {intros.riccione && <p class="shop-intro">{intros.riccione}</p>}
  <Slideshow slides={riccione} label="the Riccione store" />

  <h3>The Journey</h3>
  <p>
    Achievements. Funny moments. Good memories
  </p>
  <Slideshow slides={journey} label="building the company" />

  <h2>Whimtrade</h2>
  <span class="label">Internal operations system · 2022–2024</span>
  <p>
    Running four stores on spreadsheets stopped working somewhere around store two. Whimtrade
    was the answer: an internal system covering stock, allocation between sites, daily sales reporting, built in partnership with an external software house.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was the product owner rather than the engineer. I mapped the existing process, gathered
    requirements from staff, wrote the workflow and design documentation, worked with the
    developers through build and testing, then rolled it out across all four locations —
    training, user documentation, and the feedback loop afterwards.
  </p>
  <p>
    The idea was to turn this project, which was born for our company, into a reliable SaaS for all the second-hand related business. We never managed to go to market and it went down with the company. But the process knowledge
    and the feeling that a product like mine didn't exist on the market never left me.

  <h2>Rebuilding it, alone</h2>
  <span class="label">In progress · 2026</span>
  <p>
    I am rebuilding Whimtrade as a standalone product for second-hand retail, this time on my
    own, using LLM-assisted development. The feature set is different — it is informed by what
    actually got used in production and what did not.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interesting part is not the product. It is that the same job which previously required
    an external development team is now something one person with domain knowledge can attempt.
    I am keeping notes on where that claim holds and where it quietly breaks: where model output
    needs real review, where a deterministic solution is safer, and how much of the work turns
    out to be specification rather than code.
  </p>

  <h2>TheSneakersBase</h2>
  <span class="label">First venture · 2020–2021</span>
  <p>
    Where it started, at 18: sourcing, pricing, authenticating and shipping rare sneakers and
    limited-edition streetwear, later supplying wholesale to physical and online retailers.
    Small, unglamorous, and the reason everything after it was possible.
  </p>
</Base>
